<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="type" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="seq" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="flow_id" attr.type="long" />
  <key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="service" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="graph" attr.name="graph_usable" attr.type="boolean" />
  <key id="d3" for="graph" attr.name="categories" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d2" for="graph" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="link" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="directed">
    <node id="0">
      <data key="d5">Developer</data>
      <data key="d6">UserConsumerDeveloper</data>
      <data key="d7">Developers create code and push it to their Git repositories. Everything to be deployed is defined in the Git repository (infrastructure as code).</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1">
      <data key="d5">Git server</data>
      <data key="d6">ThirdParty</data>
      <data key="d7">Git repositories holding application code and deployment definitions. Webhooks are connected to Lagoon.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2">
      <data key="d5">EKS (Lagoon)</data>
      <data key="d6">EKS</data>
      <data key="d7">Runs Lagoon, the open source application delivery platform, and the hosting environments provided to developers. Lagoon listens to the Git repositories via webhooks and decides what to do (e.g., deploy a feature branch or a pull request environment) in the hosting zones. Operators on the cluster detect storage needs and provision databases, exposing them back to the project as a set of secrets. Migrated from OpenShift to Kubernetes for more flexibility and to stay on the bleeding edge.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3">
      <data key="d5">EC2</data>
      <data key="d6">EC2</data>
      <data key="d7">Normal EC2 instances running the whole base infrastructure / base workload of the EKS clusters.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4">
      <data key="d5">EC2 Spot</data>
      <data key="d6">EC2</data>
      <data key="d7">Spot instances used for development / non-productive infrastructure that can tolerate failure or termination of a pod. Cost benefit of 70-90% versus regular EC2. Plan to move more workloads from EC2 to Spot.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5">
      <data key="d5">Aurora R6G</data>
      <data key="d6">Aurora</data>
      <data key="d7">Provisioned when the developer explicitly asks for a database: during deployment an operator picks up the request and provisions the database, exposing it back to the project as secrets. Runs on Graviton2 r6g instances, first adopted on non-production with no regressions seen, now moving more production workloads. Stores the developers' application data.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="6">
      <data key="d5">EBS GP3</data>
      <data key="d6">EBS</data>
      <data key="d7">Used when the workload needs specialized storage, e.g., a database or a search server such as OpenSearch or Solr. gp3 volumes adopted mid-December 2020 right after announcement for lower storage cost and better IOPS, rolled out during maintenance windows. Stores the developers' application data.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="7">
      <data key="d5">EFS</data>
      <data key="d6">EFS</data>
      <data key="d7">General file storage for normal assets that need nothing specific, chosen automatically by the operator on EKS. Stores the developers' application data.</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="0" target="1" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Developers create code and push it to their Git repositories.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="2" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Webhooks notify Lagoon, which listens to the repositories and decides what needs to be done (feature branch, pull request).</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="3" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">EKS deploys the base workload onto normal EC2 instances.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">EKS deploys development / non-productive workloads onto EC2 Spot instances (70-90% cost benefit).</data>
      <data key="d10">2'</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Specialized workloads (database, search server such as OpenSearch or Solr) store data on EBS gp3 volumes.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="7" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Workloads store normal file assets in EFS.</data>
      <data key="d10">0'</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Workloads use the Aurora (r6g) database provisioned by the operator when the developer asks for a database; access is exposed as secrets.</data>
      <data key="d10">0''</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Non-production specialized workloads store data on EBS gp3 volumes.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="7" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Non-production workloads store normal file assets in EFS.</data>
      <data key="d10">1'</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Non-production workloads use the Aurora (r6g) database provisioned by the operator; access is exposed as secrets.</data>
      <data key="d10">1''</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <data key="d0">amazee.io Lagoon: Open Source Application Delivery Platform on Amazon EKS</data>
    <data key="d1">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90rWUjKjnAE</data>
    <data key="d2">amazee.io built Lagoon, an open source application delivery platform that helps developers move applications into Kubernetes without deep Kubernetes knowledge. Developers push code to Git; webhooks notify Lagoon (running on EKS), which deploys environments (feature branches, pull requests) to hosting zones on EKS backed by EC2 and EC2 Spot. Operators on EKS provision storage transparently (EFS, EBS gp3) and Aurora databases (Graviton2 r6g). Next challenges: move more from EC2 to Spot, adopt more r6g instances, and lower costs with Savings Plans.</data>
    <data key="d3">Containers, DevOps, Compute, Storage, Cost Optimization</data>
    <data key="d4">True</data>
  </graph>
</graphml>
